title: Privacy and trust
tags: Contents

!!! Reading a wiki

A published wiki is a static page and the TiddlyWiki core beside it. It loads no script, font, image or tracker from anywhere else, unless its authors wrote one into its content. Orivon checks every file of it against its IPFS address.

!!! Editing

The editor asks for no account and no name. Your copies, the keys of the services you publish with and the keys of your wikis' addresses are kept in your browser only. A wiki's own scripts and plugins run in a sandbox that cannot read them.

In Orivon the editor asks, on its first visit, for permission to make connections: to HTTPS services (the ones you publish with, IPFS gateways, free pinning), to IPFS nodes, to the IPFS node it runs on this computer, and to accept connections from the network for that node. Turned off, the editor still edits and still downloads a wiki as a CAR file; it cannot host.

The editor connects to something in these cases only, all started by you or by a switch you left on:

* ''Publish'' sends the wiki to the service you chose, under your key. The service sees your address on the internet, like any site you visit. With free pinning and hosting on, in Orivon, your page's own IPFS node connects to Orivon's node, which reads the wiki from it and pins it on Orivon's Filebase account; with hosting off, or in another browser, the wiki is uploaded to Orivon's server instead. Orivon's node sees your address on the internet, and Orivon writes down only the wiki's address, when it was pinned and its size.
* ''Hosting'' (''Settings'', under ''Publishing'', on by default in Orivon) keeps your page's IPFS node running while the editor is open, and has it announce the newest version of each wiki you published on the IPFS network. IPFS peers, visitors and Orivon's relay see your IP address: the nodes that look a wiki up, anyone who opens it, and Orivon's relay, which your node uses when your router refuses connections and which keeps no address of anyone. Turn the switch off and the node stops announcing at once.
* ''Publish'' under a wiki address also sends the signed record that points the address at the version to Orivon's node, whichever service pins the wiki. The record holds the address, the version and a signature, never a key. Orivon's node reads the version and checks it is a DecentraWiki wiki, keeps the address up for 30 days, and writes down the address, its record, the version and the time, never who sent it.
* ''Import'' from an IPFS address fetches the wiki from public IPFS gateways, which see what you fetch. Every block is checked against the address, so a gateway can fail but cannot change the wiki. Import from an IPNS address first asks Orivon's node and two public gateways for the address's record, which they see; a record counts only if it is signed by the address's key.

The key of an address never leaves your browser by itself. The key file you download is yours to keep: whoever holds it can change what the address shows.

Pressing a wiki's pen sends nothing over the network: the wiki is handed to the editor inside your browser.

!!! What you publish

Everything in a published version is public, and IPFS keeps no record of who published it. A version cannot be called back once others have copied it, so leave out what should stay private.